---
.title = "Loam: durable objects",
.description = "How glowcap keeps objects, and the capabilities they hold, across a restart, told through one shared task board.",
.date = .date("2026-10-03T00:00:00"),
.layout = "page.shtml",
.draft = false,
---

Picture a task board shared over CapTP. Your program hosts the board, its
columns, and its cards as live objects. Bob holds a reference to the "to do"
column. Carol keeps a sturdyref to the board.

Then your program restarts, and every one of those references points at
nothing.

Saving the cards' text is easy. The hard part is the references: which column
holds which card, and which object Bob was holding. A capability is a live
connection, not a value you can write down.

**Loam saves the objects together with the references between them.** After a
restart the same objects come back and the same references reach them. Objects
nobody is calling stay on disk, so the board can be far bigger than memory.

Loam is a library inside your program, not a database server. Its full API is
in Zig, under `glowcap.loam`; C and Rust get a narrower profile, and Rust's
also runs in the browser on IndexedDB ([limits]($link.ref('limits'))).
[Concepts]($link.page('concepts')) covers references and sturdyrefs if they
are new to you.

# Objects are records, references are edges

Each object is a **record**: its data, plus **edges** pointing at other
records. "The to-do column holds card #12" is an edge. An edge names a record,
not a memory address, which is why it survives a restart.

A **strong** edge keeps its target alive; a **weak** one doesn't. A **root**
is kept no matter what. So the rule for what to keep is simple: anything you
can reach from a root along strong edges.

[Kept: the board is a root and holds two columns strongly, and both columns hold the same card. Reclaimed: a preview that only a weak edge reaches, and an old card and an old column that point at each other but hang from no root.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-loam-graph.svg'))

Everything else is garbage, even two records that point at each other.
**Collection** deletes it in small batches you schedule.

Changes are **transactions**. Moving a card touches two columns, and both
change or neither does, so a crash never leaves the card in both or in
neither. Saving a transaction is called **committing** it.

# Objects sleep until called

Most cards on a big board go untouched for months, so Loam lets them
**sleep**: their state stays on disk, and every reference to them keeps
working. A call wakes the card, runs it, saves the result, and only then
replies.

[A call to a sleeping card: the Host admits it, Loam asks your worker for the record, the card's code proposes new state, your worker saves it, and only then does the reply go back.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-loam-wake.svg'))

The reply waits for the save so nobody is told about a change a crash could
undo. A crash can still land between the save and the reply, so the change
is kept but the caller never hears back. If the caller retries, the change
happens twice. Write methods that are safe to repeat, or use managed access,
which keeps receipts for its changes.

Notice the worker. Loam decides what to read and write but never touches the
disk itself, the same way glowcap's protocol core never opens a socket. Your
worker does the I/O, on threads you choose, against LMDB on disk or an
in-memory store for tests.

[Six layers from top to bottom. Peers call your Host and Vat over CapTP. The Loam attachment wakes actors and saves their state before replying. The Store keeps records, transactions and a bounded cache, and decides every read and write. Your storage worker runs that work on your threads against LMDB, IndexedDB or memory.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-loam-layers.svg'))

Calls travel down from peers to the disk, and replies travel back up. The two
Loam layers decide what happens; the two below them are yours and do the
work. The **attachment** is the piece that joins a store to
the Host you already run.

A card's code is a plain function from its saved record and a call to a
reply. It can propose new state, and it does no I/O, because a proposal that
fails to save is thrown away and an email already sent could not be:

```zig
fn invoke(call: *hosting.Call, state: core.RecordView,
    verb: []const u8, args: []const driver.Value) !driver.Value
```

# References survive a restart

To keep Carol's sturdyref working, the board gets a **publication**: a durable
secret name, the swiss number at the end of her sturdyref, saved in an index
on disk.

[Three moments over one store. While the program runs, every change is committed to disk. When it stops, nothing is left in memory, but the store still holds every record and the board's name. After the restart, Carol's fetch wakes the board, her next call wakes the to-do column, and everything else stays asleep on disk.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-loam-restart.svg'))

After a restart nothing loads until somebody asks. Carol's fetch finds the
name and wakes the board alone, and the column she asks for next wakes after
it. Your program only has to come back with the same identity key.

References inside a record work the same way. A reference to an object on
another machine can be saved only if you already hold a sturdyref for it.
Loam stores that sturdyref but can't make one for you. A reference that can't be
found again, like an unresolved promise, is refused before anything is
saved.

# Sharing with people

Usually, sharing is just handing over a reference. Some applications also need
to see who has access and change it later. **Managed access** keeps that
record for you, beside the objects.

[Alice's board and its columns sit inside one grant's scope. Bob holds the use reference and reads a column. Alice holds the control reference for the same grant and revokes it, and Bob's next read is refused.]($image.siteAsset('diagram-loam-access.svg'))

A **grant** gives permission over a resource and what sits below it. Bob gets
a **use reference**, like a ticket. Alice keeps a separate **control
reference**, like a remote, that can pause, resume, or revoke the grant. Bob
can use his access but not change it.

If Bob shares onward, the new grant sits under his and is never wider. Every
use is checked against all the grants above it, so revoking Bob's grant cuts
off everyone he shared with. Objects that don't use managed access pay
nothing for it.

# [Limits]($section.id('limits'))

- Timers, unresolved promises, subscriptions, and handles to outside
  resources can't be saved.
- [Performance]($link.page('performance').ref('persistent-actors-in-loam'))
  charts call latency, memory and restart time from one machine.
- Recovery is tested by killing the process mid-write, not by cutting power.
  The [evidence table](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/stability.md)
  lists every Loam check.
- Loam's full API is in Zig. C and Rust get a narrower profile through
  `libglowcap_loam` and Rust's `loam` feature: create a resource, share it
  with separate controls, edit it and the references it holds, restart, and
  fetch the same durable name again. Compiled for the browser, the same Rust
  feature stores to IndexedDB. TypeScript, Python, and Racket have no Loam
  yet.

[What ships](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/shipped.md#resource-models)
tracks what is built.

# Reading the whole story

Chapter 07,
[durable card](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/examples/zig/07_durable_card.zig),
runs this page's restart as one short program: it moves a card, shuts
everything down, and reopens the store to find the move still there.

The guides below go from the model to every contract. Read them in order to
understand Loam top to bottom, or jump to the one you need.

1. [Loam guide](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/loam.md):
   the model, a walkthrough of chapter 07, the words used throughout, and how
   durable actors run.
2. [Stores, transactions and reads](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/loam/store.md):
   commits, the cache, and recovering a write whose outcome is unknown.
3. [Durable names](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/loam/publications.md):
   how a sturdyref finds its actor after a restart.
4. [Durable capability fields](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/loam/references.md):
   saving references to other actors and to objects on other machines.
5. [Collection](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/loam/collection.md):
   deleting unreachable records in small batches while the program runs.
6. [LMDB operations](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/loam/lmdb.md):
   sizing, growing, compacting, and measuring the store on disk.
7. [Managed access](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/loam/access.md):
   grants, use and control references, revocation, and exact retries.
8. [C and Rust](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/loam/c-and-rust.md)
   and [the browser](https://github.com/jimisaacs/glowcap/blob/main/docs/loam/browser.md):
   the same resources from other languages.
